# The X-Pulse pulse-sequence compiler accepts ONLY Windows CRLF line
# endings. An LF-saved sequence fails to compile, with no useful error
# message (see LWG_1D-Image-Echo_H.py's changelog item 10g for the
# debugging story, and the pulse-programme-parameters skill, rule 1).
#
# "-text" tells git never to convert these files in either direction, so
# the CRLF bytes committed here are the exact bytes checked out on every
# platform -- including a Windows clone, where Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true and would otherwise rewrite them -- and the exact
# bytes GitHub serves for a raw download, which is often how a sequence
# reaches the instrument PC.
#
# The alternative spelling, "text eol=crlf", would store LF in the repo and
# convert on checkout. Correct for a clone, but a raw download would then
# deliver LF and silently fail to compile. Do not switch to it.
#
# Diffs stay textual -- this is "-text", not "binary".
#
# Deliberately NO "* text=auto" line: a catch-all would mark every existing
# file for renormalisation, and nothing outside sequences/ is compiled by
# the instrument, so there is nothing to gain and a large accidental diff
# to lose.
sequences/**/*.py -text
